# Antichain on Trees generator for arXiv:1705.01240

> **Current status:** the generator passes G1--G8 and the local size/effort part of
> G9, but it is not yet shippable. The required external oracle runs could not start:
> OpenRouter returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` on every retry.
> The harness-owned error transcript is retained; it is not counted as evidence of
> hardness.

| Profile field | Value |
|---|---|
| Track | **A — structural hardness** |
| Native domain / regime | combinatorics / finite discrete |
| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
| Certificate | integer tuple: one representative per colour |
| Objects | rooted ACT gadget tree; elements with costs in `{0,1,infinity}`; incomparable assignment |
| Intuition | reduction recognition: Start/Choose/Cover exclusions encode a multicoloured clique |
| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 3, Lemma 3 and its Appendix proof |

## What the family asks

Jones, Lafond, and Scornavacca’s [*Consistency of orthology and paralogy constraints in
the presence of gene transfers*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1705.01240) introduces
**Antichain on Trees (ACT)** as the intermediate problem in its hardness proof. An
instance supplies a rooted tree, named elements, and finite-cost placements. The solver
must place every element so that no two selected tree nodes are ancestor-related and the
total cost meets a bound.

This module uses exactly the paper’s Lemma 3 Start/Choose/Cover/count gadgets. The
instance is rendered compactly by giving its colour classes and pairwise compatibility
matrices plus the complete gadget grammar. The answer lists one graph vertex from each
colour. The checker expands those representatives into every placement in the paper’s
construction, then checks finite weights, exact total weight, node uniqueness, and
root/child incomparability. It accepts every valid witness and never reads the planted
answer.

## Why it may be hard

Theorem 1, through Lemmas 3--5, proves NC NP-hard and TMNC W[1]-hard; Lemma 4 proves
ACT NP-hard and MWACT W[1]-hard even with costs only `0`, `1`, and infinity and the
placement restrictions used here. The generator samples one representative per colour
first and makes every colour-pair graph regular of degree about `3n/5`, conditioned on
the planted edge. Thus every planted and decoy vertex has the same degree into every
other colour. Independent relabellings and degree-preserving two-switches remove the
initial cyclic presentation.

The easy regimes matter. Theorem 2 gives the paper’s dynamic program in
`O(2^k k! k |D| |N|^4)` time when `k` is maximum degree of the least-resolved DS-tree;
it is polynomial for binary DS-trees. Theorem 3 makes the unknown-highway,
non-minimizing variant polynomial. This family uses the high-degree Lemma 3 ACT image,
and the colour count grows from 14 to 18 across the non-demo ladder. At shipping size,
MRV backtracking with forward checking exhausted 200,000 nodes on 8/8 seeds, averaging
3,076,364 compatibility updates and 1.32 seconds. This is distributional evidence, not
a proof of average-case hardness; the paper’s theorem alone would not establish Track A.

## Worked demo

For `make_instance(n=3, classes=3, degree=2, seed=7)`, the rendered data portion is:

```text
Colour classes (in answer-slot order):
  C1: 6 8 4
  C2: 9 5 3
  C3: 7 1 2

Compatibility matrices:
  PAIR C1 C2       PAIR C1 C3       PAIR C2 C3
    1: 101           1: 011           1: 110
    2: 110           2: 110           2: 101
    3: 011           3: 101           3: 011
```

The complete renderer also defines every gadget and placement. A valid answer is
`<answer>[8,9,1]</answer>`:

```python
verify(inst, [8, 9, 1])
# (True, "ok")
verify(inst, [4, 9, 1])
# (False, "representatives 4 and 9 are incompatible")
```

The demo has 9 valid answers among 27 correctly shaped candidates and is readily
solvable on paper. It is illustrative only and never ships.

## Presets and gates

| Preset | class size `n` | colours | cross-degree | status |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
| demo | 3 | 3 | 2 | hand-scale illustration |
| easy | 40 | 14 | 24 | hardening start |
| medium | 48 | 16 | 29 | ladder |
| hard | 56 | 18 | 34 | intended shipping preset, pending oracle evidence |

| Gate | Measured result |
|---|---|
| G1 | 12/12 planted witnesses verified; all answers JSON-round-trip |
| G2 | 6/6 corruptions rejected with six distinct reasons |
| G3 | prose + fenced tagged JSON round-tripped and verified |
| G4 | 0/200,000 structure-aware guesses; space `56^18` |
| G5 | shipping density 0/200,000; exact attack averaged 200,001 nodes, 3,076,364 updates, 1.32 s |
| G6 | triangle outlier, greedy, 64 restarts, spectral, exact MRV: each 0/8 |
| G7 | doubling class size 56→112 builds and verifies with the answer fixed at 18 labels |
| G8 | 40/40 relabelling checks and 40/40 carried witnesses; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
| G9(c) | 73 chars, about 19 tokens, 18 atoms; 153 intended compatibility inspections |
| G9(b) | **pending**: external oracle service rejected all calls before inference |

The five local attacks deliberately include construction-aware statistics. The
triangle score is stronger than degree (which is exactly constant); greedy maximizes
future compatible domains; the restart variant randomizes ties; the spectral probe runs
centred power iteration; and the domain-standard exact attack uses MRV plus forward
checking.

## Oracle and G9 evidence

The bare harness attempted `easy` but obtained only service errors:

| Arm/preset | Calls | Solved | Result |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| bare / easy | 4 retries | 0 usable | HTTP 403 key total-limit; no verdict |
| hinted / hard | 4 retries | 0 usable | HTTP 403 key total-limit; no verdict |
| placebo / hard | 4 retries | 0 usable | HTTP 403 key total-limit; no verdict |

There is therefore no meaningful hinted-minus-placebo estimate. The files
`g9_hinted_transcript.jsonl` and `g9_placebo_transcript.jsonl` retain those isolated,
harness-owned error records. Once a funded OpenRouter key is available, rerun the bare
ladder, then rerun both copies in their separate scratch directories. The structural
hint merely names the multicoloured-clique image; it does not give an algorithm or a
derived value.

## Use

```python
import gen_1705_01240 as gen

params = gen.DIFFICULTY[gen.SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY]
inst = gen.make_instance(seed=12345, **params)
question = gen.render(inst)
candidate = gen.parse_answer("work... <answer>[1,2,3]</answer>")
ok, reason = gen.verify(inst, candidate)
```

After the oracle evidence exists, emit from the repository root with:

```bash
bash scripts/emit.sh 1705.01240 20 hard
```

## Caveats

- The NP-hardness theorem is worst-case. It does not prove this planted regular
  distribution hard; the attack panel supplies finite empirical evidence only.
- G4 samples uniformly from assignments already containing exactly one listed vertex
  per colour. Zero hits does not bound a graph-aware heuristic’s success probability
  and is not, by itself, a statistical proof that the true rate is below `1e-6`.
- The exact search has a declared 200,000-node cutoff. Optimized SAT/CP-SAT, MILP/SDP,
  branch-and-bound clique code, belief propagation, and stronger multi-eigenvector or
  semidefinite planted-subgraph methods were not tested.
- The 564,590-character shipping statement is large. Oracle failure could partly
  measure representation burden; the answer itself is only 18 labels.
- `canonical_key` uses the sorted exact four-cycle counts of all colour-pair graphs.
  It is invariant under all generated relabellings and separated 20/20 tested seeds,
  but it can theoretically over-collapse nonisomorphic adversarial instances.
- The witness is a paper-licensed compact encoding of the full ACT assignment, not a
  literal gene-tree reconciliation. Lemma 5 licenses the next reduction to an LGT
  network, but this module stops at the ACT object and makes no claim of native
  phylogenetic-domain coverage.

