+# Acyclic orientations with two-sided degree constraints
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **A — structural hardness** |
+| Native domain | `logic` |
+| Object regime | `finite_discrete` |
+| Computational core | `csp_sat` |
+| Certificate form | `integer_tuple` |
+| Domain essentiality | `licensed_reduction` |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed: Section 3.1, Theorem 10 |
+| Intended intuition | constraint propagation: coordinate each complementary literal pair's early/late choice so every clause gadget sees both sides |
+
+This generator implements the reduction in Király and Pálvölgyi, [*Acyclic orientations with degree constraints*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1806.03426), Theorem 10. The solver receives the paper's complete skeleton/literal/clause multigraph in an indexed representation. It must give a signed Boolean assignment, the proof's compact encoding of a topological order in which every nonterminal vertex has at least two incident edges to earlier vertices and two to later vertices. `verify` expands that order, constructs every multiedge, and recomputes all predecessor and successor counts with integers. It never reads the planted answer.
+
+## Why this is the hard regime
+
+Theorem 10 proves NP-completeness for exactly the fixed `k = l = 2` regime used here. The generator samples an assignment first and builds a connected regular NAE-3-SAT formula around it, then applies the theorem's reduction. Every variable has six occurrences at the shipping preset, split evenly between positive and negative literals, while clauses are balanced between one and two planted-true literals. Thus the certificate is known by inverse generation, without solving the emitted instance, and the most immediate planting statistics are neutralized.
+
+The paper's easy cases are deliberately excluded: Theorem 2 gives a greedy algorithm with only indegree lower bounds; Theorem 3 is polynomial when `f(v)g(v)=0` at every vertex; Theorem 4 is greedy when all vertices are strict; and Theorem 9 reduces `k = l = 1` to biconnectivity. The shipping distribution instead uses Theorem 10's two-sided, non-strict gadgets. This is a Track A claim about the audited distribution, not a claim that worst-case NP-completeness alone proves average-case hardness. At `n=174`, bounded NAE-DPLL hit 4,001 nodes after 4,261,268 clause inspections without a witness; all five attacks failed on 8/8 independent seeds.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+The demo (`n=6`, seed 0) renders the following full instance:
+
+```text
+ACYCLIC ORIENTATION WITH TWO-SIDED DEGREE CONSTRAINTS
+
+A multigraph may contain parallel edges but no loops.  An ordering of its
+vertices orients every edge from the earlier endpoint to the later endpoint,
+and is therefore acyclic.  Edge multiplicity counts toward degree.
+
+Here n=6 and m=4.  The instance has Boolean variables x1,...,x6
+and clauses C1,...,C4.
+A positive literal xi has the value of xi; a negative literal ~xi has the
+opposite value.  A clause is NAE-satisfied when its three literal values are
+not all equal.
+
+The multigraph is specified by the following complete construction (all
+indices below are inclusive, and these are all its edges):
+1. Skeleton vertices are a0,...,a33.  Put two parallel edges
+   a(2i)--a(2i+1) for i=0,...,16, and one edge
+   a(2i-1)--a(2i) for i=1,...,16.
+2. For i=1,...,6, yi is adjacent to a(2i-1), a(2i), xi, and ~xi.
+3. For j=1,...,4, Cj is adjacent to a(2n+2j-1), a(2n+2j),
+   and to the three literal vertices displayed in clause j below.
+4. For i=1,...,6, zi is adjacent to
+   a(2n+2m+2i-1), a(2n+2m+2i), xi, and ~xi.
+5. For every literal vertex xi and ~xi, put two parallel edges from a0
+   to it and two parallel edges from it to a33.
+
+The designated source is s=a0 and sink is t=a33.
+A witness must make every other graph vertex have at least two incident
+edges to earlier vertices and at least two incident edges to later vertices;
+parallel edges are counted separately.
+
+Your compact witness chooses each variable's truth value.  It denotes the
+following ordering: a0 first; each true literal vertex is placed immediately
+after a1; yi, then Cj, then zi occupy their indexed gaps in the skeleton;
+each false literal vertex is placed immediately before the last even-indexed
+skeleton vertex; and the final odd-indexed skeleton vertex is last.  Within
+either literal block, increasing variable index is used.  The checker builds
+this full order and checks every multiedge and degree bound exactly.
+
+Clauses (variables and clause numbers are 1-indexed):
+C1: x1 x4 ~x5
+C2: x5 ~x6 ~x2
+C3: ~x1 ~x3 ~x4
+C4: x3 x6 x2
+
+Output exactly 6 signed integers in a JSON list, in variable order.
++i means xi=true and -i means xi=false; absolute values must therefore
+be exactly 1,2,...,6.  The first sign may be either sign, because
+globally complementing all values preserves every NAE clause.
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags.
+Syntax example for a hypothetical four-variable instance:
+<answer>[-1,2,-3,4]</answer>
+For this instance your list must contain all 6 entries.
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+One witness is `<answer>[-1,-2,3,-4,-5,-6]</answer>`. It gives `verify(...) == (True, "ok")`. Dropping its final entry gives `(False, "wrong length: expected 6 entries, got 5")`. This smallest setting is intentionally hand-solvable: there are only 32 assignments after quotienting the global-complement symmetry, and 13 work.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
